BT /F1 10 Tf 72 720 Td (Python Basics for Complete Beginners) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (This short guide introduces the first ideas every new Python programmer needs: storing values) Tj 0 -13 Td (in variables, working with numbers and text, making decisions with if statements, and repeating) Tj 0 -13 Td (work with loops. Each part ends with a small example you can type and run yourself.) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (Variables and Assignment) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (A variable is a name that refers to a value. You create one by writing the name, an equals) Tj 0 -13 Td (sign, and the value. Python works out the type of the value for you, so you never declare it.) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (age = 16) Tj 0 -13 Td (name = "Priya") Tj 0 -13 Td (height_in_metres = 1.62) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (The equals sign does not mean "is equal to" here. It means "store the value on the right under) Tj 0 -13 Td (the name on the left". You can change what a name refers to at any time:) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (score = 10) Tj 0 -13 Td (score = score + 5) Tj 0 -13 Td (print\(score\)) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (This prints 15, because the second line reads the old value of score, adds 5, and stores the) Tj 0 -13 Td (result under the same name.) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (Good variable names describe what they hold. Use lowercase letters and underscores, such as) Tj 0 -13 Td (total_price or number_of_students. Names cannot start with a digit and cannot contain spaces.) Tj 0 -13 Td (Python treats Score and score as two different names, so be consistent.) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (Numbers and Arithmetic) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (Python has two main kinds of numbers. Integers are whole numbers such as 3 or -40. Floats are) Tj 0 -13 Td (numbers with a decimal point, such as 2.5 or 0.1. The usual operators work as you would expect:) Tj 0 -13 Td (+ adds, - subtracts, * multiplies and / divides.) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (Division with / always gives a float, even when the answer is whole: 10 / 2 gives 5.0. When you) Tj 0 -13 Td (want only the whole-number part of a division, use //, and when you want the remainder, use %:) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (minutes = 135) Tj 0 -13 Td (hours = minutes // 60) Tj 0 -13 Td (left_over = minutes % 60) Tj 0 -13 Td (print\(hours, "hours and", left_over, "minutes"\)) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (This prints 2 hours and 15 minutes. The ** operator raises a number to a power, so 2 ** 3 gives) Tj 0 -13 Td (8.) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (Floats are stored in binary, so some decimal values cannot be represented exactly. For example,) Tj 0 -13 Td (0.1 + 0.2 gives 0.30000000000000004. When you show a result to a person, round it with) Tj 0 -13 Td (round\(value, 2\).) Tj 0 -13 Td ET
