BT /F1 10 Tf 72 720 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (Working with Text) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (Text values are called strings. You can write them with single or double quotes; both mean the) Tj 0 -13 Td (same thing. Joining two strings with + is called concatenation:) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (first = "Ada") Tj 0 -13 Td (last = "Lovelace") Tj 0 -13 Td (full_name = first + " " + last) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (You cannot add a number directly to a string. Writing "Age: " + 16 raises a TypeError. Convert) Tj 0 -13 Td (the number first with str\(16\), or use an f-string, which lets you place values inside text by) Tj 0 -13 Td (putting them in curly braces:) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (age = 16) Tj 0 -13 Td (message = f"{full_name} is {age} years old") Tj 0 -13 Td (print\(message\)) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (Strings have many useful methods. name.upper\(\) gives the text in capital letters, name.strip\(\)) Tj 0 -13 Td (removes spaces from both ends, and len\(name\) gives the number of characters. Strings are also) Tj 0 -13 Td (sequences, so name[0] is the first character and name[-1] is the last one.) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (Making Decisions with if) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (An if statement runs a block of code only when a condition is true. The condition is followed) Tj 0 -13 Td (by a colon, and the block underneath must be indented, usually by four spaces. Python uses the) Tj 0 -13 Td (indentation, not brackets, to know which lines belong to the block.) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (temperature = 31) Tj 0 -13 Td (if temperature > 30:) Tj 0 -13 Td (    print\("It is hot today."\)) Tj 0 -13 Td (elif temperature > 20:) Tj 0 -13 Td (    print\("It is warm today."\)) Tj 0 -13 Td (else:) Tj 0 -13 Td (    print\("Take a jacket."\)) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (Python checks the conditions from top to bottom and runs only the first block whose condition) Tj 0 -13 Td (is true. elif means "else if" and you can have as many as you need. The else block runs when) Tj 0 -13 Td (none of the conditions are true.) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (Conditions use comparison operators: == for equal, != for not equal, and <, >, <= and >= for) Tj 0 -13 Td (ordering. A common beginner mistake is writing = instead of == inside a condition; = stores a) Tj 0 -13 Td (value, while == compares two values. You can combine conditions with and, or and not, for) Tj 0 -13 Td (example age >= 13 and age <= 19.) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (Repeating Work with Loops) Tj 0 -13 Td () Tj 0 -13 Td (Loops let you repeat code without writing it again. A for loop goes through the items of a) Tj 0 -13 Td (sequence one at a time:) Tj 0 -13 Td ET
